WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.850
POS, cards and ATMs: pay, withdraw and fix failed payments.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.960
This is the video for module 3 of Digital Payments and POS Basics.

3
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:17.840
POS means point of sale. The machine asks your bank for permission, and your bank approves or declines.

4
00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:23.780
Most problems come from rushing, letting someone else handle your card, or not checking the amount.

5
00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:33.720
You will learn a safe card routine, how to read a receipt, how to use an ATM, how float works, and what to do when a payment fails.

6
00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:42.200
For example, Blessing in Owerri agrees a total of 18,400 naira for gas and groceries.

7
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:49.800
She inserts the card herself, chip first. At a filling station, ask for the terminal to be brought to you.

8
00:00:51.050 --> 00:01:03.730
The screen shows 184,000. She counts the digits: six, but 18,400 has five. She does not enter her PIN. She asks the cashier to enter it again.

9
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:11.970
Now it shows 18,400. She covers the keypad with her other hand and enters her PIN.

10
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:21.500
The receipt says APPROVED. She checks the name and last four digits on the card she gets back, because a thief may swap a similar card.

11
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:29.790
Never say your PIN aloud or let a cashier type it. If someone may have seen it, change it as soon as you can.

12
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:42.450
For example, Ruth's supermarket payment of 23,750 naira prints a DECLINED receipt, then an APPROVED one. Only APPROVED means money went through.

13
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:54.800
She checks her app before leaving and sees one debit, not two. She keeps both receipts, because the terminal ID, RRN and STAN are what her bank needs to trace a payment.

14
00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:02.660
Kemi's app showed a separate charge line for each withdrawal. So she listed what she really needs cash for each week.

15
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:11.360
Transport, about 7,000 naira. Market items, about 10,000. Small extras, about 3,000.

16
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:24.680
That is 20,000. Now she makes one withdrawal on Monday from her own bank's ATM, and keeps half at home so she does not carry it all. Read any fee notice on the screen before you confirm.

17
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:33.610
Use an ATM at a bank branch, in daylight. Never accept help from a stranger. If you need help, go inside the bank.

18
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:41.520
If the machine keeps your card, do not walk away. Call your bank's saved number from where you stand and block the card.

19
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:52.990
Before you insert your card, look at the slot and keypad. If anything looks loose or glued on, use another machine. And always take your card first, then your cash.

20
00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:03.620
Float is cash in your drawer plus money in your agent account. Zainab starts with 150,000 cash and 100,000 in her account.

21
00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:11.970
When a customer withdraws, her cash goes down and her account goes up. When a customer gives cash to send, the opposite happens.

22
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:19.790
After three customers she has 110,000 in cash and 140,000 in her account.

23
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:30.450
Still 250,000 in total. Her charges of 2,400 sit in a separate envelope, so she knows exactly what she earned.

24
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.620
Never give out cash until the terminal prints APPROVED and your app shows the credit.

25
00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:48.200
For example, Ebere in Aba tries to withdraw 30,000 naira at a POS agent. It prints DECLINED, but she gets a debit alert. She does not let the agent try again.

26
00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:56.850
She checks her app for a reversal and photographs the declined receipt with its terminal ID, RRN and STAN.

27
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:09.620
That evening there is still no reversal, so she calls her own bank's number from the back of her card. Only your own bank can reverse a debit on your account. She writes down the complaint reference.

28
00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:25.150
She checks daily until the 30,000 returns. If your bank does not resolve it, escalate within the bank, then to the CBN's consumer protection channel. Real banks never ask for your PIN or OTP.

29
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:41.860
To remember. Count the digits before your PIN. Only APPROVED means you paid. Never accept help at an ATM. Float is cash plus account balance. And when a payment fails, do not repeat it. Call your own bank with your receipt.

30
00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:53.050
Circle the key fields on a real receipt, practise the six card checks, plan one weekly withdrawal, and build your complaint kit with your bank's official contacts.

31
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.340
Continue with the lessons below.
